{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — confirmed root cause (Phase 1 complete)\n\nTwo mechanisms, both required to explain the symptom. Confirmed by runtime observation, not\nby reading source.\n\n### (a) `enrich()` sends an unbounded prompt — the call can never succeed\n\n`enrich()` assembles `title + description + every comment body` (`enrich.py:27-44`) and hands\nit to the runner as `RunRequest(instructions=source, ...)` (`enrich.py:96-105`) with no size\nbound anywhere on the path.\n\nMeasured today against this store:\n\n| ticket | assembled chars |\n|---|---|\n| `8625-7bea-67db-4cdc` | 8,539,143 |\n| `9305-b42c-3262-4c58` | 6,166,714 |\n| `e27c-5a20-cd13-43b2` | 2,992,643 |\n\nInstrumented the runner seam: `enrich()` puts **2,992,643 characters** on the wire for\n`e27c` against a 200,000-token `claude-haiku-4-5` window. Real provider call, today, 2.4 s:\n\n```\nstatus_code: 400 ... 'prompt is too long: 206826 tokens > 200000 maximum'\n  -> rebar.llm.errors.LLMUnavailableError\n```\n\n### (b) `drain()` discards a disposition the codebase has ALREADY computed as non-retryable\n\nThis is the mechanism that turns a failure into a permanent wedge, and it is sharper than\n\"drain has no permanent branch\".\n\n`run_failure.py:152-165` raises the `LLMUnavailableError` **and attaches a classified\ndisposition to it**: `provider_err.outcome = classify_llm_failure(...)`. For this exact error\nthe classifier maps status 400 + the literal hint `\"prompt is too long\"`\n(`failure.py:148-157`, `failure.py:235-237`) to `ResolutionClass.CHANGE_INPUT`, whose message\nis *\"The input was rejected (too large / malformed)\"* and whose `retryable` is **False**.\n\nObserved on the real failure (`getattr(exc, \"outcome\")`):\n\n```\nLLMOutcome(resolution_class=<ResolutionClass.CHANGE_INPUT: 'CHANGE_INPUT'>, retryable=False)\n```\n\n`enrich_drain.drain` then catches it with a blanket `except Exception` and applies the\ntransient posture unconditionally (`enrich_drain.py:284-287`): *\"will retry after lease\"*, no\n`DONE_ENRICH`. The queue's reducer has no terminal-failure state, so the lease expires, the\nentry returns to `pending`, and the next drain re-claims it.\n\nThe disposition rebar computed — *do not retry this* — is thrown away one stack frame above\nthe code that needed it. `run_failure.py`'s own comment anticipates this consumer: *\"the\nper-seam wiring + exit-code use is story blackbear's\"*. The drain is a seam that was never\nwired.\n\n### Runtime proof of the cycle (the `dynamic` claim, observed — not read)\n\nLocal ephemeral store, one enqueued ticket, a runner raising the verbatim provider error,\nfive drain cycles with the clock advanced past each lease:\n\n```\ncycle 0: drain={'processed': 0, 'batch': 5} state={'claimed':True,'pending':False,'done':False} enrich_calls=1\ncycle 1: drain={'processed': 0, 'batch': 5} state={'claimed':True,'pending':False,'done':False} enrich_calls=2\ncycle 2: drain={'processed': 0, 'batch': 5} state={'claimed':True,'pending':False,'done':False} enrich_calls=3\ncycle 3: drain={'processed': 0, 'batch': 5} state={'claimed':True,'pending':False,'done':False} enrich_calls=4\ncycle 4: drain={'processed': 0, 'batch': 5} state={'claimed':True,'pending':False,'done':False} enrich_calls=5\nFINAL: done=False  still in pending_enrichment=True  total provider calls=5\n```\n\nNever terminal, one wasted provider call per lease period, forever.\n\n### A rival hypothesis, tested and NOT the cause — but worth recording\n\n`pending_enrichment` returns ids **sorted**, and `drain` stops at `overlap_drain_batch` (5)\n*successes*, so a deep backlog starves ids late in sort order. This store currently has\n**898 pending entries out of 4,903 tickets**, and the three wedged ids sit at indices\n**458 / 499 / 786**. So today the batch cap alone would keep them from being reached.\n\nIt is not the root cause — they *were* observed being claimed and failing (2026-08-20 16:02\nand 16:17), and the 400 reproduces on demand — but two consequences follow and should not be\nmistaken for this bug:\n\n- **The `enrich drain gate check took ~Nms` warning has a different dominant cause.** With\n  898 pending, `pending_enrichment` clears the gate marker on every probe\n  (`queue.py:374-375`), so `958e`'s O(1) fast path can never engage and every write pays the\n  full scan. Fixing this bug will not silence that warning; draining the 898-entry backlog\n  will. Treating the warning as this bug's oracle would be wrong.\n- **AC #3 (\"the three entries reach `done`\") is gated on backlog position, not on this fix.**\n\n### Defect attribution (`caused_by`)\n\nBoth mechanisms are **omissions**, not regressions — no commit made working behaviour wrong;\na bound and a permanent branch were never written. The date test agrees: the implicated lines\nare from 2026-07-09 and the behaviour became wrong later, as the store's largest tickets grew\npast the window. So attribution is by **scope of work**, not by introducing commit.\n\n- `enrich.py:84-105` (the unbounded assembly and the `RunRequest`) — `git blame -w -C -M`\n  gives `047293e9f17` (2026-07-09), `rebar-ticket: ee3d-fc7b-e280-4cbb`, the story that\n  introduced `enrich()`. **This is the attributed origin**: the op that reads a ticket of\n  unbounded size and calls a fixed-window model is where the missing bound belonged.\n- `enrich_drain.py:279-287` (the transient-only posture) came from `9ad46965bdc`\n  (2026-07-09), `rebar-ticket: c1de-d6a0-6cef-4135`. Recorded for the reader, but not the\n  attributed origin: a transient-retry posture is correct *in general*; what makes it\n  unrecoverable here is that the input can never fit, which is `ee3d`'s omission.\n\nDivergence from the mechanical default, stated per protocol: a whole-file blame majority\nwould also land on `047293e9f17`, so the attribution agrees with the default — but by\nscope-of-work reasoning, not because blame found an introducing change.\n\n### Legitimacy\n\n`defect`. The authoritative source is rebar's own classification: `failure.py` already\ndeclares this failure `retryable=False` / `CHANGE_INPUT`, and `run_failure.py` deliberately\nattaches that disposition for seams to consume. A seam that retries it forever contradicts\nrebar's own stated contract for the error it is handling."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787295615931642001,"uuid":"8c1bb0ea-180f-44ac-aa4c-c04cfdebdd3a"}